(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_chemistry_redoxreactions=[{id:"neet-2026r-q077",pn:77,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Oxidation number",q:"The correct decreasing order of oxidation state of the underlined atom in each molecule is",o:["N\u0332\u2082O\u2085 > A\u0332l\u0332\u2082O\u2083 > H\u2082S\u0332","P\u0332b\u0332O\u2082 > N\u0332\u2082O\u2083 > S\u0332O\u2083","P\u0332\u2084O\u2086 > C\u0332l\u0332\u2082O\u2087 > A\u0332l\u0332H\u2083","P\u0332\u2084O\u2081\u2080 > S\u0332O\u2083 > H\u2082O\u0332"],c:0,s:`Underlined atoms: option 1 N, Al, S; option 2 Pb, N, S; option 3 P, Cl, Al; option 4 P, S, O.
P\u2084O\u2086: P = +3, Cl\u2082O\u2087: Cl = +7, AlH\u2083: Al = +3.
P\u2084O\u2081\u2080: P = +5, SO\u2083: S = +6, H\u2082O: O = \u22122.
N\u2082O\u2085: N = +5, Al\u2082O\u2083: Al = +3, H\u2082S: S = \u22122.
PbO\u2082: Pb = +4, N\u2082O\u2083: N = +3, SO\u2083: S = +6.
Only option 1 (+5 > +3 > \u22122) is in decreasing order.
Note: the underlining is hard to see in the printed options; the underlined atoms are taken from the publisher's solution.`},{id:"neet-2025-q055",pn:55,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Oxidation number",q:`Consider the following compounds :
KO\u2082, H\u2082O\u2082 and H\u2082SO\u2084 (the underlined elements are K in KO\u2082, O in H\u2082O\u2082 and S in H\u2082SO\u2084)
The oxidation state of the underlined elements in them are, respectively,`,o:["+1, \u22121, and +6","+2, \u22122, and +6","+1, \u22122, and +4","+4, \u22124, and +6"],c:0,s:`KO\u2082: an alkali metal always shows +1, so K is +1.
H\u2082O\u2082: oxygen is \u22121 (each H is +1 in H\u2013O\u2013O\u2013H).
H\u2082SO\u2084: sulphur is +6.`,fig:{w:210,h:30,alt:"KO\u2082, H\u2082O\u2082 and H\u2082SO\u2084 with K, the O of H\u2082O\u2082 and the S of H\u2082SO\u2084 underlined.",d:[["t",4,18,"K",""],["t",13,18,"O\u2082,",""],["l",4,22,12,22,""],["t",64,18,"H\u2082","e"],["t",64,18,"O",""],["t",73,18,"\u2082",""],["l",64,22,73,22,""],["t",100,18,"and","m"],["t",145,18,"H\u2082","e"],["t",145,18,"S",""],["t",153,18,"O\u2084",""],["l",145,22,153,22,""]]},sfig:{w:220,h:194,alt:"H\u2013O\u2013O\u2013H with +1 on each H and \u22121 on the oxygen; H\u2082SO\u2084 with S (+6) double bonded to two O (\u22122) and single bonded to two OH groups (O \u22121, H +1).",d:[["t",4,34,"H\u2082O\u2082",""],["t",42,34,"\u2192",""],["t",66,14,"+1","s"],["t",66,32,"H",""],["l",76,28,94,36,""],["t",101,41,"O","m"],["l",108,37,128,37,""],["t",135,41,"O","m"],["t",141,31,"\u2212","s"],["l",142,41,158,50,""],["t",164,58,"H","m"],["t",84,54,"\u22121","s"],["t",164,71,"+1","m s"],["t",4,142,"H\u2082SO\u2084",""],["t",46,142,"\u2192",""],["c",140,128,10,""],["t",140,132,"S","m"],["t",118,120,"+6","s"],["l",142,118,142,94,""],["l",138,118,138,94,""],["t",140,90,"O","m"],["t",147,82,"\u22122","s"],["l",138,138,138,162,""],["l",142,138,142,162,""],["t",140,175,"O","m"],["t",140,187,"\u22122","m s"],["l",131,133,114,146,""],["t",108,152,"O","m"],["t",96,138,"\u22121","s"],["l",102,154,88,164,""],["t",82,172,"H","m"],["l",149,133,166,146,""],["t",172,152,"O","m"],["t",170,138,"\u22121","s"],["l",178,154,192,164,""],["t",198,172,"H","m"]]}},{id:"neet-2024-q052",pn:52,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Identifying redox reactions",q:"Which reaction is NOT a redox reaction?",o:["H\u2082 + Cl\u2082 \u2192 2HCl","BaCl\u2082 + Na\u2082SO\u2084 \u2192 BaSO\u2084 + 2NaCl","Zn + CuSO\u2084 \u2192 ZnSO\u2084 + Cu","2KClO\u2083 + I\u2082 \u2192 2KIO\u2083 + Cl\u2082"],c:1,s:`(1) H goes 0 \u2192 +1 and Cl goes 0 \u2192 \u22121. This is a redox reaction.
(2) Ba stays +2, Cl stays \u22121, Na stays +1, SO\u2084 stays \u22122. No oxidation state changes, so it is not a redox reaction.
(3) Zn goes 0 \u2192 +2 and Cu goes +2 \u2192 0. This is a redox reaction.
(4) I goes 0 \u2192 +5 (oxidation) and Cl goes +5 \u2192 0 (reduction). This is a redox reaction.`},{id:"neet-2023-q089",pn:89,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Balancing redox reactions",q:`On balancing the given redox reaction,
aCr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B + bSO\u2083\xB2\u207B(aq) + cH\u207A(aq) \u2192 2aCr\xB3\u207A(aq) + bSO\u2084\xB2\u207B(aq) + (c)/(2) H\u2082O(\u2113)
the coefficients a, b and c are found to be, respectively-`,o:["8, 1, 3","1, 3, 8","3, 8, 1","1, 8, 3"],c:1,s:`Use the ion-electron method.
Reduction: Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B + 6e\u207B \u2192 2Cr\xB3\u207A.
Oxidation: SO\u2083\xB2\u207B \u2192 SO\u2084\xB2\u207B + 2e\u207B, multiplied by 3.
Add: Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B + 3SO\u2083\xB2\u207B \u2192 2Cr\xB3\u207A + 3SO\u2084\xB2\u207B.
Balance O by adding 4H\u2082O on the right, then balance H by adding 8H\u207A on the left.
Cr\u2082O\u2087\xB2\u207B + 3SO\u2083\xB2\u207B + 8H\u207A \u2192 2Cr\xB3\u207A + 3SO\u2084\xB2\u207B + 4H\u2082O.
So a = 1, b = 3, c = 8.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q078",pn:78,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Decomposition redox reaction",q:"Which of the following reactions is a decomposition redox reaction ?",o:["P\u2084(s) + 3OH\u207B(aq) + 3H\u2082O(l) \u2192 PH\u2083(g) + 3H\u2082PO\u2082\u207B(aq)","2Pb(NO\u2083)\u2082(s) \u2192 2PbO(s) + 4NO\u2082(g) + O\u2082(g)","N\u2082(g) + O\u2082(g) \u2192 2NO(g)","Cl\u2082(g) + 2OH\u207B(aq) \u2192 ClO\u207B(aq) + Cl\u207B(aq) + 4H\u2082O(l)"],c:1,s:`A decomposition reaction has one reactant breaking into two or more products.
Lead nitrate breaks into PbO, NO\u2082 and O\u2082.
Nitrogen goes from +5 to +4 (reduced).
Oxygen goes from -2 to 0 (oxidised).
So it is a decomposition as well as a redox reaction.
The others are disproportionation or combination reactions.`},{id:"neet-2021-q075",pn:75,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Displacement reactions",q:"Which of the following reactions is the metal displacement reaction? Choose the right option.",o:["2Pb(NO\u2083)\u2082 \u2192(\u0394) 2PbO + 4NO\u2082 + O\u2082\u2191","2KClO\u2083 \u2192(\u0394) 2KCl + 3O\u2082","Cr\u2082O\u2083 + 2Al \u2192(\u0394) Al\u2082O\u2083 + 2Cr","Fe + 2HCl \u2192 FeCl\u2082 + H\u2082\u2191"],c:2,s:`Reactions 1 and 2 are decomposition reactions (one substance splits).
In reaction 4 a metal displaces hydrogen, not a metal.
In reaction 3 Al displaces the metal Cr from its oxide.
So (3) is a metal displacement reaction.`},{id:"neet-2020-q136",pn:136,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Redox Reactions",st:"Oxidation number",q:"What is the change in oxidation number of carbon in the following reaction ? CH\u2084(g) + 4Cl\u2082(g) \u2192 CCl\u2084(l) + 4HCl(g)",o:["0 to + 4","\u2013 4 to + 4","0 to \u2013 4","+ 4 to + 4"],c:1,s:`In CH\u2084: C + 4(+1) = 0, so C = \u22124.
In CCl\u2084: C + 4(\u22121) = 0, so C = +4.
The change is \u22124 to +4.`}]})();
